TITLE:: RLTransform
summary:: A transformation of an RLChordContext's chords
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLChordContext, Classes/RLWalk, Classes/RLChord, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
A transformation sends each chord of an link::Classes/RLChordContext:: to a chord: code::chord -> left * chord * right::, with strong::left:: and strong::right:: two elements of the context's group. The left element moves every chord the same way (a transposition or an inversion of the pitch classes); the right one is read from the chord itself (P, L, R and their products). The two sides commute.

Transformations are usually made by the context (code::ctx.t(7)::, code::ctx.l::, code::ctx.define::) and composed with code::<>::, strong::right to left:: as functions do: code::(l <> p).(chord):: applies p first. A word, the list of a walk's steps, is written the other way, in the order of time (link::Classes/RLWalk::).

A transformation is a value, and its equality is about what it does: two pairs are equal when they move every chord alike, whatever their labels. In 12 steps T6 (on the left) and RPRP (on the right) are equal.

code::
~ctx = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.triads(RLEDO(12)));
~h = ~ctx.l <> ~ctx.p <> ~ctx.l;            // LPL, the hexatonic pole
~h.(~ctx.space.major(0));                    // Ab minor
(~h <> ~h).isIdentity;                       // true
(~ctx.t(5) <> ~ctx.r) == (~ctx.r <> ~ctx.t(5));     // true: the two sides commute
(~ctx.r <> ~ctx.p).repeated(2) == ~ctx.t(6);        // true
(~ctx.l <> ~ctx.r).repeated(12).isIdentity;         // true in 12 steps
~ctx.l.inverse == ~ctx.l;                    // true: P, L and R undo themselves
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: context
argument:: left
An element code::[v, e]:: of the context's group; the identity when nil.
argument:: right
The same, for the right side.
argument:: label
A String, or nil.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: context
METHOD:: left
METHOD:: right
METHOD:: label

METHOD:: value
The chord a chord goes to. code::transform.(chord)::.

METHOD:: images
The same as a list of one chord.

METHOD:: relates
Whether it takes x to y.

METHOD:: sourceIdentity
METHOD:: targetIdentity
METHOD:: follows
What an link::Classes/RLCategory:: asks of its arrows: the transformation that moves nothing, and whether this can come after that (the same context).

METHOD:: <>
This after that.

METHOD:: inverse

METHOD:: repeated
n times itself.

METHOD:: isIdentity
Whether it leaves every chord in place.

METHOD:: isLeft
METHOD:: isRight
Whether it can be written on that side alone: a T or an I; a product of P, L, R.
